WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.927
Before we use the larger practice database, this slide gives us a very small example that we can reuse to

00:00:08.927 --> 00:00:10.082
understand all four major join types.

00:00:10.082 --> 00:00:17.837
On the left, the patients table has Ana with ID 1, Ben with ID 2, and Cara with ID 3.

00:00:17.837 --> 00:00:25.037
On the right, the admissions table contains two admissions for patient 1, one admission for patient 3,

00:00:25.037 --> 00:00:27.427
and one admission for patient 4.

00:00:27.427 --> 00:00:31.502
Patient 4 does not exist in our tiny patient table.

00:00:31.502 --> 00:00:37.042
This small mismatch is intentional because it lets us see what each join preserves.

00:00:37.042 --> 00:00:41.052
With an INNER JOIN, we keep only matching combinations.

00:00:41.052 --> 00:00:50.207
Ben disappears because patient 2 has no admission, and admission 104 disappears because patient 4 has no

00:00:50.207 --> 00:00:51.857
patient row.

00:00:51.857 --> 00:00:58.187
With a LEFT JOIN, every patient remains, so Ben stays and the admission columns for Ben become NULL.

00:00:58.187 --> 00:01:06.577
With a RIGHT JOIN, every admission remains, so admission 104 stays and its patient columns become NULL.

00:01:06.577 --> 00:01:10.937
With a FULL OUTER JOIN, both unmatched cases remain.

00:01:10.937 --> 00:01:13.267
Use this as your prediction habit.

00:01:13.267 --> 00:01:20.146
Before you run the SQL, ask which unmatched rows should survive and where NULL values should appear.
